Docker Fundamentals – Week 1
Day 2 – Docker Images, Containers & Docker Hub
🎯 Today's Goal

By the end of today, you'll be able to:

✅ Understand Docker Images
✅ Understand Docker Containers
✅ Pull images from Docker Hub
✅ Run Ubuntu and Nginx containers
✅ Access a container interactively
✅ Understand the container lifecycle
✅ Answer common interview questions
1. What is a Docker Image?

A Docker image is a read-only template that contains everything needed to run an application:

Application code
Runtime
Libraries
Dependencies
Configuration

Think of it like this:

Recipe (Image)
      ↓
Cook the recipe
      ↓
Prepared Dish (Container)

One image can create many containers.

Example:

Ubuntu Image
      ↓
Container 1

Ubuntu Image
      ↓
Container 2

Ubuntu Image
      ↓
Container 3
2. What is a Docker Container?

A container is a running instance of an image.

For example:

Image
Ubuntu

↓

docker run ubuntu

↓

Ubuntu Container

A container is isolated from other containers but shares the host's operating system kernel.

3. What is Docker Hub?

Docker Hub is the public repository where Docker images are stored.

Examples of official images:

ubuntu
nginx
mysql
redis
alpine
node
python
tomcat

You don't need to create these images yourself—you can download them.

4. Pull Your First Image

Download the Ubuntu image:

docker pull ubuntu

Expected output:

Using default tag: latest

latest: Pulling from library/ubuntu

Downloaded newer image for ubuntu:latest

List downloaded images:

docker images

Example output:

REPOSITORY   TAG      IMAGE ID       CREATED      SIZE

ubuntu       latest   xxxxxxxxx      2 weeks ago  78MB
5. Run an Ubuntu Container
docker run ubuntu

What happens?

Docker creates a container.
The container has no long-running process.
It exits immediately.

Check:

docker ps

You'll probably see no running containers.

Now check all containers:

docker ps -a

Example:

CONTAINER ID

STATUS

Exited (0)
6. Run Ubuntu Interactively

Start an interactive shell:

docker run -it ubuntu bash

You'll see:

root@container_id:/#

Now you're inside the container.

Try these commands:

pwd
ls
whoami
hostname
cat /etc/os-release

Exit the container:

exit
7. Download and Run Nginx

Pull the image:

docker pull nginx

Run it in the background:

docker run -d nginx

The output is a long container ID.

Check running containers:

docker ps

Example:

CONTAINER ID

IMAGE

STATUS

PORTS

The Nginx container keeps running because its web server process stays active.

8. Give Your Container a Name

Instead of using the container ID:

docker run -d --name webserver nginx

Check:

docker ps

Now you'll see:

NAMES

webserver

This makes container management much easier.

9. Container Lifecycle
docker run
      ↓
Created
      ↓
Running
      ↓
Stopped
      ↓
Removed

Useful commands:

Start:

docker start webserver

Stop:

docker stop webserver

Restart:

docker restart webserver

Remove:

docker rm webserver
10. Enter a Running Container

Open a shell inside the Nginx container:

docker exec -it webserver bash

If bash isn't available:

docker exec -it webserver sh

Inside the container, try:

pwd
ls
hostname
ps

Exit:

exit
11. View Container Logs

Check what a container is doing:

docker logs webserver

Follow logs in real time:

docker logs -f webserver

This is a common troubleshooting step in interviews and production.

12. Inspect a Container

See detailed configuration:

docker inspect webserver

This includes:

IP address
Network settings
Mounted volumes
Environment variables
Image details
13. Summary of Important Commands
Command	Purpose
docker pull ubuntu	Download Ubuntu image
docker images	List images
docker run ubuntu	Create and run a container
docker run -it ubuntu bash	Interactive Ubuntu shell
docker pull nginx	Download Nginx
docker run -d --name webserver nginx	Run Nginx in background
docker ps	Running containers
docker ps -a	All containers
docker stop webserver	Stop container
docker start webserver	Start container
docker restart webserver	Restart container
docker exec -it webserver bash	Enter running container
docker logs webserver	View logs
docker inspect webserver	Detailed container information
Real-World Scenario

Imagine an application team says:

"Our website is down."

As a DevOps engineer, your first checks might be:

docker ps
docker logs webserver
docker inspect webserver
docker exec -it webserver bash

These commands help you determine whether the container is running, whether the application inside it has errors, and how it is configured.

